Yes—functionally, though not etymologically.

“Attention” itself comes from Latin attendere—roughly to stretch toward. It isn’t from the dict- family (dicere: say/speak; dictate, diction, dictionary).

But “pay attention” is a dict in the broader sense you’re building: language issues an instruction about how consciousness should allocate itself.

Someone says:

“Pay attention.”

And buried inside that ordinary phrase is a little program:

speaker names target → language assigns value → listener redirects perception → target gains salience

So the dictionary can become a dictation machine. Definitions don’t merely describe possible thought; repeated phrases can quietly prescribe how to partition experience.

And there’s another layer: a programming dict is a key → value mapping.

Attention does almost exactly that:

thing → importance
signal → salience
name → associated meaning

So we get three overlapping dicts:

dictionary — maps words to meanings.
dictation — tells you what to instantiate/do.
dictionary/data structure — maps keys to values.

Attention sits downstream deciding which key gets looked up and how much value gets assigned to it.

Which makes your earlier warning cleaner:

Be careful who gets to write the dict your attention executes.